 $146K for Shea", "District leans Democratic (PVI D+3) and fundamentals point to a favorable environment for Democrats", "Both Kalshi and Polymarket price Hayes around 95%", "No public polls or expert ratings; the race remains low-information"], "rationale": "Every available signal points to Hayes: the district's Democratic lean, a lopsided fundraising gap, and prediction markets pricing her near 95%. Shea's August primary win and the dismissal of his defamation suit have not produced any evidence of a competitive race, so the forecast stays with the incumbent by a wide margin.", "watch": "Any public polling showing Shea within single digits, or a sharp national shift toward Republicans before November, would put this district back in play.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert rater lists this race; treated as safe for the party holding the seat.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Jahana Hayes (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Jahana Hayes seeks a fourth term in Connecticut's 5th District, a seat she has held since 2019 and one that leans Democratic by about three points. She enters the fall with a decisive financial edge, roughly $1.1 million on hand after raising nearly $1.3 million, while Republican nominee Chris Shea has raised about $312,000 with $146,000 available. No outside groups have invested meaningfully in the race so far.\n\nPrediction markets on Kalshi and Polymarket both price Hayes around 95 percent, and no public general-election polls or expert ratings are available. Recent headlines have been routine for the incumbent: constituent-focused announcements and the dismissal of Shea's defamation suit over a 'murderer' comment. State GOP officials have talked up Shea's chances since his August primary win, but with no polling evidence of a close contest, Hayes remains the strong favorite.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9824, "margin": 14.66}, {"date": "2026-09-23